[1] In 1816, the Scottish scientist David Brewster was studying how light behaves when it reflects off glass. [2] While experimenting with two mirrors placed at an angle, he noticed that small objects between {{1|them was repeated}} in a symmetrical pattern. [3] Brewster built a tube around the mirrors, {{2|adding colored glass at one end, and}} called the device a kaleidoscope, from Greek words meaning "beautiful form" and "to look at."

[4] {{3|Brewster patented the device in 1817, however, his patent}} had a flaw. [5] A mistake in the paperwork left the design poorly protected, and copies appeared in shops {{4|almost immediately}}. [6] Within months, an enormous number of {{5|kaleidoscopes was sold}} in London and Paris. [7] Brewster{{6|, however}} earned little from them. {{7|}}

[8] Although he was disappointed, Brewster continued his research on light, which earned him many scientific honors. [9] Today he is remembered less for that research than for a toy {{8|that he hoped would also be useful to artists and designers}}.
